Experiment 7 – Type-2 Hypervisor & VM Image

Aim:
To demonstrate virtualization by installing a Type-2 hypervisor and creating/configuring a Windows or Linux VM image.

Procedure:

Open the Type-2 virtualization software.
Install the hypervisor on the host computer.
Launch the hypervisor application.
Select Create New Virtual Machine.
Select the required Windows or Linux ISO image.
Specify the virtual machine name and location.
Configure the required virtual hardware.
Create the virtual machine.
Start the VM and complete the OS installation.
Verify that the VM is running successfully.

Input:
Windows/Linux ISO image and VM configuration details.

Output:
A configured virtual machine running the selected operating system.

Result:
Thus, the Type-2 hypervisor was installed and a virtual machine was successfully created and configured.

Experiment 8 – Virtual Machine Configuration

Aim:
To create a virtual machine with 1 CPU, 2 GB RAM and 15 GB storage using Type-2 virtualization software.

Procedure:

Open the Type-2 virtualization software.
Select Create New Virtual Machine.
Enter the virtual machine name.
Select the required OS/ISO image.
Set the CPU to 1 processor.
Allocate 2 GB RAM.
Create a virtual disk of 15 GB.
Review the VM configuration.
Create and start the virtual machine.
Verify the allocated CPU, RAM and storage.

Input:
CPU = 1, RAM = 2 GB, Storage = 15 GB.

Output:
A virtual machine configured with 1 CPU, 2 GB RAM and 15 GB storage.

Result:
Thus, the virtual machine was successfully created with the specified resources.

Experiment 9 – Virtual Hard Disk using VMware

Aim:
To create a virtual hard disk and allocate storage using VMware Workstation.

Procedure:

Open VMware Workstation.
Select the required virtual machine.
Open the VM settings.
Select Add Hardware.
Select Hard Disk.
Select the required virtual disk type.
Specify the required storage capacity.
Select the disk location.
Create and save the virtual hard disk.
Verify that the disk is attached to the VM.

Input:
Virtual disk type, storage capacity and disk location.

Output:
A virtual hard disk with the specified storage allocation.

Result:
Thus, the virtual hard disk was successfully created and allocated using VMware Workstation.

Experiment 10 – VM Snapshot and Restore

Aim:
To create a snapshot of a virtual machine and test it by loading the previous version.

Procedure:

Open the virtualization software.
Select the required virtual machine.
Start the VM and make a configuration change.
Open the Snapshot option.
Create a new snapshot.
Give a suitable snapshot name.
Make another change to the VM.
Select the previously created snapshot.
Choose Restore/Revert to the snapshot.
Verify that the previous VM state is restored.

Input:
Virtual machine state and snapshot name.

Output:
The VM is restored to its previously saved state.

Result:
Thus, the VM snapshot was successfully created and the previous version was restored.

Experiment 11 – VM Clone

Aim:
To create a clone of a virtual machine and test it by loading the cloned VM.

Procedure:

Open the virtualization software.
Select the existing virtual machine.
Open the Clone option.
Select the appropriate cloning option.
Enter a name for the cloned VM.
Select the storage location.
Create the clone.
Wait for the cloning process to complete.
Start the cloned virtual machine.
Verify that the cloned VM works correctly.

Input:
Source VM, clone name and storage location.

Output:
A new virtual machine with the same configuration as the source VM.

Result:
Thus, the virtual machine was successfully cloned and the cloned VM was tested.

Experiment 12 – Change VM Hardware Compatibility

Aim:
To change the hardware compatibility of an already created and configured virtual machine.

Procedure:

Open the virtualization software.
Select the existing virtual machine.
Open the VM settings/options.
Select Change Hardware Compatibility.
Choose the required compatibility version.
Review the compatibility settings.
Confirm the compatibility change.
Complete the configuration process.
Start the virtual machine.
Verify that the VM operates with the new hardware compatibility.

Input:
Existing VM and required hardware compatibility version.

Output:
The VM is updated to the selected hardware compatibility.

Result:
Thus, the hardware compatibility of the virtual machine was successfully changed.
